{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "07", "section": "03", "chapter_title": "Productivity and Workflow Automation", "title": "Template-Based Productivity", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting template-based productivity to privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation", "cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/07/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_calibration"]}, "content": "# Template-Based Productivity\n\n![Template-Based Productivity textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-03-template-based-productivity.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA template is the difference between doing a task once and doing it once for every future time. The student who retypes a budget total every term, or hand-formats a weekly status note from scratch, is paying a tax that compounds. The student who builds the calculation as a formula and saves the blank shell as a reusable file pays the cost once. This section is about the mechanics that make a document reusable rather than disposable: live formulas instead of typed values, cell references that copy correctly, and a master file you never edit directly.\n\nThe skill matters most in spreadsheets, where the gap between a typed number and a formula is the gap between a document that goes stale the moment you change an input and one that stays correct forever. Understanding relative versus absolute references is the single highest-leverage spreadsheet idea a freshman can learn.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Replace hand-typed totals with formulas (`=SUM`) so values recalculate when inputs change.\n- Distinguish relative references (`B2`) from absolute references (`$C$1`) and predict how each behaves when copied.\n- Keep a blank master template separate from each filled-in working copy.\n- Justify a template by the recurring task it eliminates, not by how the document looks.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student builds a course-budget spreadsheet, types the total `250` into the bottom cell, and submits it. A week later the lab fee changes from \\$45 to \\$60. The student updates the lab-fee cell, but the total still reads `250`, because it was a typed number, not a formula. The spreadsheet now contradicts itself, and nobody notices until grading.\n\nThe lesson is that a typed value is a snapshot frozen at the moment you typed it, while a formula is a living relationship that updates with its inputs. Every recurring document should be built from living relationships.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTemplate-based productivity rests on two mechanics.\n\nThe first is the **formula**: a cell whose value is computed from other cells rather than typed. `=SUM(B2:B4)` does not store a number; it stores an instruction to add the range, recomputed every time any cell in it changes. This is why a budget built with formulas can never go stale: the total is defined as \"whatever the parts add up to,\" not \"250.\"\n\nThe second is the **reference type**, which controls what happens when you copy a formula. This is the concept students most often get wrong:\n\n| Reference | Written as | When copied down a column | Use it for |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Relative | `B2` | Shifts to `B3`, `B4`, ... | The value on each row |\n| Absolute | `$C$1` | Stays locked at `C1` | A shared constant like a tax rate |\n| Mixed | `$C1` or `C$1` | Locks only the part with `$` | Locking a column but not a row |\n\nA formula like `=B2*$C$1` says \"this row's cost times the one fixed tax rate.\" Copy it down and `B2` becomes `B3`, `B4` (each row's own cost) while `$C$1` stays pinned to the single rate cell. Get the dollar signs wrong and the formula either drifts off the rate or fails to advance through the rows.\n\nThe third practice is **separating master from working copy**: keep `budget_template.xlsx` blank and reusable, and Save As a dated copy (`budget_2026spring.xlsx`) before filling it in, so the template survives for next term.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is a real spreadsheet template turned from a retyped document into a reusable one.\n\n**Before (a copied report with a hand-typed total):**\n\n```\n       A            B\n1  Item         Cost\n2  Books        120\n3  Lab fee      45\n4  Software     85\n5  TOTAL        250        <- typed by hand, now wrong after edits\n```\n\nIf you change the lab fee to 60, cell B5 still says 250. The value is stale.\n\n**After (fixed structure, formulas, and a locked rate):**\n\n```\n       A            B            C\n1  Item         Cost         Tax rate: 0.08\n2  Books        120          =B2*$C$1\n3  Lab fee      60           =B3*$C$1\n4  Software     85           =B4*$C$1\n5  TOTAL        =SUM(B2:B4)   =SUM(C2:C4)\n```\n\nThree concrete moves made it a template:\n\n1. **Formula instead of a typed total.** `=SUM(B2:B4)` recalculates the moment a cost changes.\n2. **Relative and absolute references.** `=B2*$C$1` copies down so B shifts to B3, B4 while the tax rate stays locked at `$C$1`.\n3. **Edit a copy, keep the master blank.** Save As `budget_2026spring.xlsx` so `budget_template.xlsx` stays reusable next term.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict what each reference does when you copy the formula down\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Cell C2 holds =B2*$C$1. You copy it down into C3. The B2 part is a relative reference. What does it become?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It stays B2\",\"It becomes B3\",\"It becomes C2\",\"It becomes $B$3\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Relative references shift with the copy, so B2 becomes B3, picking up each row\u2019s own cost.\"},{\"prompt\":\"In that same copied formula, the $C$1 part is an absolute reference. What does it become in C3?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It becomes $C$2\",\"It becomes C1\",\"It stays locked at $C$1\",\"It becomes $D$1\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"The dollar signs pin both the column and row, so $C$1 stays locked on the single tax-rate cell no matter where you copy it.\"},{\"reveal\":\"If you had written =B2*C1 with no dollar signs, copying down would give =B3*C2, =B4*C3, and the rate reference would drift off C1 entirely and produce garbage. Lock the shared constant with $C$1.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"You want a percentage cell to recalculate automatically every time you enter a new graded item. Should it hold a typed number or a formula?\" explanation=\"A typed value is a snapshot frozen at the moment you typed it; a formula like =SUM(B2:B10)/SUM(C2:C10) is a living relationship that updates with its inputs. Results get formulas.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A formula, so it recomputes whenever an input changes\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A typed number, because it is faster to enter\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A typed number locked with dollar signs\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Either one works identically\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you type a number into a spreadsheet, ask whether it is an input or a result. Inputs (costs, the tax rate) get typed once; results (totals, taxes) get formulas. Before you copy a formula, ask which references should move and which should stay, and add `$` to the ones that must stay.\n\nA reusable document passes three checks:\n\n1. **Formula check:** Is every computed cell a formula, so nothing goes stale when an input changes?\n2. **Reference check:** When you copy a formula, do row references shift and shared constants stay locked with `$`?\n3. **Master check:** Is the blank template preserved separately from the filled-in working copy?\n\nIf a \"total\" is a typed number, the document is a one-time snapshot, not a template.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging error is typing a result you could compute. A hand-typed total looks identical to a formula on screen but silently goes wrong the first time an input changes.\n\nThe second is forgetting the dollar signs. `=B2*C1` copied down becomes `=B3*C2`, `=B4*C3`, so the tax rate reference drifts off the rate cell entirely and produces garbage. Lock the constant with `$C$1`.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply this to a weekly grade tracker. You want a sheet where you enter points earned and points possible for each assignment, and a final cell shows your current percentage. Inputs are the two point columns, typed each week. The percentage is a result: `=SUM(B2:B10)/SUM(C2:C10)`, formatted as a percent. Because it is a formula, every new graded item you enter updates your standing automatically.\n\nNow suppose each category has a weight (homework 30%, exams 70%) stored in a corner cell. Each category's weighted contribution is `=category_percent*$E$1` style, with the weight cell locked by `$` so it does not drift when you copy the formula across categories. Save the empty version as `grade_tracker_template.xlsx`; each course gets its own Save As copy. The walkthrough shows the same three mechanics (formula, locked reference, master copy) producing a tool you reuse for every class.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader clicks into a spreadsheet cell to see whether it holds a number or a formula, rather than trusting the displayed value. A total that turns out to be typed is a defect, however correct it looks today.\n\nA strong reader of a template can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which cells are inputs (typed) and which are results (formula-driven)?\n2. When this formula is copied, which references move and which stay locked, and is that what the math requires?\n3. If an input changes, does every dependent value update on its own?\n4. Where is the blank master, separate from this filled-in copy?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is a working template plus a short note on its formulas. It is strong when a reader can change any input and watch every result update correctly, and when the absolute references are placed exactly where a shared constant lives.\n\nA useful note sounds like this: \"I replaced the typed total with `=SUM(B2:B4)` and built the tax column as `=B2*$C$1` so it copies down without losing the rate. I rejected hard-coding the 0.08 rate into each formula because then changing the rate would mean editing every row instead of one cell.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the template to a context with a different constant, say a currency conversion instead of a tax rate. The structure is identical: per-row values stay relative, the conversion rate stays absolute in one cell. What changes is that exchange rates move daily, so the single locked cell now needs a date label and a habit of updating it. Recognizing that the `$`-locked-constant pattern transfers, while the maintenance discipline around the constant must adapt, shows real command of the idea.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the template-based productivity packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-03-template-based-productivity-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"reusable spreadsheet template with formulas and locked references\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the reusable spreadsheet template.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the relative-versus-absolute rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the template.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries for CAT 100 07.03. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your template passes the formula, reference, and master checks: results are formulas, shared constants are locked with `$`, row references shift correctly when copied, and the blank master is saved separately.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity", "cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"], "stem": "You open budget_template.xlsx and type this term's real numbers into it. Next week the blank template is gone. What should you have done?", "options": ["Saved a copy with File > Save As before entering data, keeping the master blank", "Typed faster so the template would not change", "Deleted the numbers after submitting", "Renamed the file to template_final.xlsx"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "Edit a copy, not the master. Save As (or an .xltx template) keeps a blank, reusable original.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_overwrite_master", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity"], "stem": "Column B holds item costs in B2:B10. Which entry in the total cell stays correct after you edit a cost?", "options": ["Type the sum 450 directly", "=SUM(B2:B10)", "Type =450", "Leave the cell blank and add costs in your head"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "=SUM(B2:B10) recalculates whenever a cost changes. A typed 450 goes stale on the next edit.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_hardcode_instead_of_formula", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity"], "stem": "A status report template should be reused weekly without retyping. What makes it a true template rather than a copied document?", "options": ["It is saved as a PDF", "Its fixed structure stays put and the parts that change are marked with placeholders like [WEEK] and [STATUS]", "It uses a colorful font", "It is emailed to yourself each week"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Placeholders flag exactly which fields to fill, so no stale value from last week is left behind.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_format_without_placeholders", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_template_based_productivity_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_template_based_productivity"], "stem": "In D2 you write a formula that multiplies each price in B2 by the tax rate in C1, then copy it down. Which formula copies correctly down the column?", "options": ["=B2*C1 (both relative)", "=B2*$C$1 (relative price, absolute tax rate)", "=$B$2*$C$1 (both absolute)", "=$B2*C1 (mixed on the wrong cell)"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "B2 must shift to B3, B4 as you copy down; the single tax rate cell must stay locked as $C$1.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_no_relative_reference", "difficulty": "hard"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_overwrite_master", "pattern": "editing_the_master_template", "description": "Typing real data straight into the master template file so the blank template is destroyed", "trigger": "learner opens budget_template.xlsx, fills in their numbers, and saves over it", "feedback": "Save a copy first (File > Save As, or duplicate the .dotx/.xltx), then edit the copy. The master should stay blank and reusable.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_hardcode_instead_of_formula", "pattern": "typed_value_instead_of_formula", "description": "Typing a computed result by hand into a spreadsheet cell instead of writing a formula that recalculates", "trigger": "learner types 450 into a total cell rather than =SUM(B2:B10)", "feedback": "Use a formula like =SUM(B2:B10) so the total updates when a number changes. A typed 450 goes stale the moment a row edits.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_format_without_placeholders", "pattern": "manual_retyping_each_time", "description": "Treating a template as a finished document to retype rather than as fixed structure with clearly marked fill-in fields", "trigger": "learner copies last week's report and edits scattered words, missing some old values", "feedback": "Mark the variable parts with placeholders like [NAME] or [DATE] so every reuse fills the same fields and nothing stale is left behind.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_3_no_relative_reference", "pattern": "misuse_of_absolute_reference", "description": "Locking a cell reference with dollar signs when the formula should adjust as it is copied down a column", "trigger": "learner copies =B2*$C$2 down and every row multiplies by the wrong cell", "feedback": "Use a relative reference (B2) for the part that should shift per row, and an absolute reference ($C$2) only for the one fixed cell like a tax rate.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}